The bug is that `Arrays.sort` sorts the arrays in place, but `char[]` doesn't override `equals`, so `first.equals(second)` falls back to `Object.equals` and compares references, which are always different. That's why even true anagrams come back false.

```java
public static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
    if (a == null || b == null) return false;
    if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
    char[] first = a.toCharArray();
    char[] second = b.toCharArray();
    Arrays.sort(first);
    Arrays.sort(second);
    return Arrays.equals(first, second);
}
```

Using `Arrays.equals` compares the contents element by element instead of the array references, so sorted anagrams match.
